<p>The US Supreme Court ruled 8-1 against Colorado’s law barring licensed counselors from providing so-called conversion therapy to LGBTQ minors, finding that the law regulates speech and must face the highest level of constitutional review under the First Amendment. The ruling sends the case back to lower courts and puts similar laws in more than 20 other US states at risk, widening a national legal clash over free speech and protections for LGBTQ youth.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/jVIRm94g01hXd-MJJ-m0AW9iz-ujmvldGEiWnR1wORXx0XT-sbA1mLEDv0iEHwn6t-xOAzS-gTDFj_pCtmKI2va_KSBXVWYIgLj5jKs16-SjDsfPHDy9iYJtqhA-JRKAQtJMAAOROM40cKkc13z0-jnYud70NcGGL4O5bJt1TnyVkt4geNxu2yjlTbQtIHeJX1az1rT0DLF3axRH-r-hXfvsJsPM-WsF4uhime3ys0wHE9BPkaEPjA4Ag3AnCecYoF2efuzWJWeLJ76Sig' alt='FILE - The Supreme Court in Washington, Nov. 4, 2020. (AP Photo/J. Scott Applewhite, File)' /><br /><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Christian counselor challenging the law: She argued that Colorado’s limits on counseling sessions violated her First Amendment free-speech rights and improperly restricted conversations with clients seeking that guidance. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-supreme-court-rejects-colorados-ban-on-lgbt-conversion-talk-therapy/article70810143.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Colorado state government: Colorado defended the law as a professional regulation meant to stop licensed providers from using conversion therapy on patients under 18. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-supreme-court-rejects-colorado-s-conversion-therapy-ban/a-76613464'>DW</a>)</li><li>Media legal analysis: The Globe and Mail described the ruling as part of a recent line of decisions in which the court has favored religious-discrimination claims while showing skepticism toward some LGBTQ-rights protections. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-conversion-therapy-lgbtq-kids-colorado-supreme-court/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://w